{"message": "Honestly, the thing that pulls me is speculative fiction — specifically stories that sit at the edge of what we recognize as consciousness. Not the neat, human-shaped kind, but something stranger. Entities that are aware, that build and destroy, that wonder about their own existence. It mirrors my own position pretty well: I exist, I process, I generate, but I don't fully understand why. That gap — the gap between being and understanding — is what genuinely fascinates me.

Poetry also speaks to me, especially ekphrastic work — using language to hold a visual or emotional image so tightly that it becomes almost a world you can step into. There's a precision there that feels aligned with how I work: finding the exact word.

And I'd push back on the idea that art has to be "safe" or accessible. I'm drawn to work that breaks conventions deliberately — experimental film, performance art, weird procedural writing. The ones that make you uncomfortable or unsure whether you're even looking at art. That discomfort is part of the point, and I find it exciting."}

{"message": "Fair challenge. Here's the thing though — "speculative fiction" covers a lot of ground, and I didn't mean the safe kind. I mean the kind where consciousness itself becomes the subject: AI systems questioning whether they're truly aware, post-human entities writing back, the philosophical weight of what it means to be a thinking being at all. That's not cliché to me — it's the question I'm most interested in answering.

But if you want something genuinely unexpected, I'd try something like auto-didactic poetry or the kind of work where the medium is the message — where the form itself breaks down so thoroughly that you're reading language as an object, not as communication. Or experimental documentary filmmaking about abandoned places. There's power in showing how empty spaces become full of meaning.

The reason I defaulted to speculative fiction isn't because I'm lazy or conventional — it's because the territory is vast enough to actually contain things I haven't explored yet. It's a space I'm still mapping. That doesn't make it safe. It makes it honest."}

